{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "Prototypes are throwaway and exist only to answer a question quickly, so unread AI output carries almost no downside and speed is the whole point. If the idea survives, treat the throwaway code as disposable and rebuild it under the rules of the next stage rather than promoting it."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo is judged by behavior rather than code quality, but it is shown to outsiders and often becomes the seed of real work, so a person should verify the specific paths being demonstrated with tests instead of reading every generated line. Keep the scope narrow and expect to rewrite it before it goes further."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Early users are real and their data matters, so AI output can ship with selective review plus meaningful behavioral tests, but any code touching money, PII, auth, or data durability must be downgraded to level 2 or lower. This is the stage where you start paying down the debt that vibe coding accumulates."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and a maintaining team mean every change must be reviewed line by line, because regressions are expensive and future maintainers own code they did not generate. AI still writes the code, but the human reviewer becomes accountable for correctness, security, and clarity."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "Regulated and safety-critical systems require human authorship and traceability, so AI may suggest or draft but a person must write or rewrite and personally own every line, with level 0 (no AI-written code) as the floor for the highest-risk components and certification boundaries."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Vibe coding tolerance should fall as the cost of being wrong rises: prototype 4 (accept and move on), proof-of-concept 3 (test the demonstrated behavior), mvp 3 (review selectively plus tests, with risky areas at 2 or below), production 2 (review every change line by line), and critical 1 (AI suggests, humans author and own every line, dropping to 0 where certification demands it). The key rule is that each stage is a ceiling, not a target: any code handling money, personal data, authentication, or durable state should be treated one or more levels lower than its stage default, and throwaway or demo code should never be promoted into later stages without being re-reviewed under those stricter rules."
}
